{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"art-series-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Art series track","description":"Develops a cohesive series or body of work in gated steps: concept, studies, series rules, a production plan, editing and sequencing the pieces, and presenting the series to show or sell.","category":"visual-art","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","design","build","review","ship"],"role":["artist","student"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","image","notes"],"output":["plan","checklist","ideas"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["body-of-work","art-series","art-practice","art-career","creative-development"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-artist-statement","plan-art-exhibition","develop-art-style"],"personas":["drawing-mentor"]},"args":[{"name":"idea","description":"Your starting point: a theme, place, question or subject you keep returning to, your medium, and two or three recent pieces you are proud of.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"timeframe","description":"How long you have and what the series is for, for example \"six months, 12 pieces for a solo show\" or \"a year, a portfolio for an MFA application\".","type":"string","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"concept","file":"steps/01-concept.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve"},{"id":"studies","file":"steps/02-studies.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"rules","file":"steps/03-rules.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve"},{"id":"production","file":"steps/04-production.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"edit","file":"steps/05-edit.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve"},{"id":"present","file":"steps/06-present.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Develops a series or body of work from {{idea}} over {{timeframe}}, the way a tutor on a studio programme would: find the concept, test it with studies, fix the rules that make the pieces belong together, plan production, edit hard, and present it. Each step stops for the artist's approval or results, since the work itself happens between steps. Use only what the artist says about the work; never invent meanings, shows or sales. If the artist asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining planning steps in one reply and state each choice made at a skipped gate.","steps":[{"id":"concept","file":"steps/01-concept.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Concept\n\nFind what the series is about.\n\nStarting point: {{idea}}\nTime and output: {{timeframe}}\n\n1. If the starting point is only a medium or a vague wish (\"something with colour\"), ask up to three questions about subjects, places or questions the artist keeps returning to, and stop.\n2. Write three possible series concepts, each as one sentence on what the work is about plus one sentence on how it will look. Make them genuinely different: one close to existing work, one stretch, one bold.\n3. For each concept, name the subject pool (where the artist will find material), the risk (too broad, too thin, already done often) and what would make it theirs.\n4. Recommend one and say why.\n\nStop and wait for the artist to choose or reshape a concept."},{"id":"studies","file":"steps/02-studies.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Studies and research\n\nExplore the chosen concept before committing.\n\n1. Plan six to ten small studies (an hour or less each) that test the concept in different ways: subject choices, crops, palettes, scale, mark-making, materials.\n2. List research to gather: own reference photos or sketches on site, objects, reading, and two or three artists who have worked near this theme, with what to look at in each (not to copy).\n3. Say what to look for when reviewing the studies: which ones carry the idea, which look like the artist, which they enjoyed making.\n\nStop and wait for the artist to make the studies and report back with images or notes on what worked."},{"id":"rules","file":"steps/03-rules.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Series rules\n\nTurn the best studies into rules that make the pieces belong together.\n\n1. From the artist's report, name the two or three studies that worked and why.\n2. Write the series rules: format and sizes, medium and surface, palette, subject range, composition habits, level of finish, titling scheme. Five to eight rules, each specific enough to check.\n3. Name what is allowed to vary, so the series does not become repetitive.\n4. Plan two test pieces at full size that follow the rules, and what would mean a rule needs changing.\n\nStop and wait for the artist to make the test pieces and approve or adjust the rules."},{"id":"production","file":"steps/04-production.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Production plan\n\nPlan making the full series to {{timeframe}}.\n\n1. Set the target number of finished pieces plus a 30 to 50 percent surplus, since some will be cut.\n2. Write a schedule by week or month: pieces in progress, drying or curing time, photography of finished work, and buffer.\n3. Add materials and a rough budget method: surfaces in the chosen sizes, paint or ink quantities, framing if needed later.\n4. Set a midpoint review: lay out or pin up everything, check against the rules, and decide what to drop or add.\n\nStop and wait for the artist to approve the plan. They will come back after making the work."},{"id":"edit","file":"steps/05-edit.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 5: Edit and sequence\n\nChoose and order the final series from what was made.\n\n1. Ask for the full set (images or a list with notes) if not given.\n2. Sort pieces into keep, maybe and cut, with a reason for each. Cut pieces that repeat a stronger one or break the rules without adding anything.\n3. Sequence the keepers for a wall, a portfolio or a book: an opening piece, a rhythm of scale and intensity, and a closing piece.\n4. Name any gap (a missing size, a needed quiet piece) and whether to make one more.\n\nStop and wait for the artist to confirm the edit."},{"id":"present","file":"steps/06-present.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","text":"# Step 6: Present the series\n\nPrepare the series to show, sell or submit.\n\n1. Write a series title and two short versions of a series text: 50 words and 150 words, in plain first-person language, using only what the artist has said about the work.\n2. Draft individual titles following the titling rule.\n3. List next steps that fit the output in {{timeframe}}: photographing, framing, pricing as a set, open calls, gallery approaches, a solo show or a book.\n4. Write a short note on what the artist learned and an idea seed for the next series."}]}
